    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i mean i get the \"universal right\" thing, but it feels kind of hollow when the actual experience for so many kids is just a failing system. if we can solve an individual's problem right now with vouchers, isn't it kind of wrong to prioritize a \"community institution\" that isn't even working for them? it feels like the opposition is just clinging to an outdated idea of how things should work instead of doing what's best for the actual people.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This highlights a core conflict between systemic reform and individual solutions. Those advocating for vouchers believe that focusing on individual efficacy—getting the kid where they can succeed—is a more immediate and effective priority. Critics counter that while individual struggles are real, relying solely on vouchers creates a fragmented system that doesn't address the deep-seated issues within the entire public education infrastructure. Ultimately, the debate boils down to whether the necessary fix is localized student relief or comprehensive, system-wide improvement.",
      "source": "target",
